a CTFont under NSFontAttributeName is legitimate

kCTFontAttributeName and NSFontAttributeName are the SAME KEY, both CFSTR("NSFont"). An attributed
string built through CoreText, which is what CTLineCreateWithAttributedString takes, therefore
carries a CTFontRef where an AppKit one carries an NSFont. On a Mac those are interchangeable; in
this port KTFont and NSFont are unrelated classes.

NSLayoutManager sends -setInContext: to whatever it finds under that key. A KTFont did not answer:

  cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[KTFont_FT setInContext:]
  cider:   chain KTFont_FT   A/CoreText
  cider:   chain KTFont      A/CoreText
  cider:   chain NSObject    lib/libobjc.A.dylib

which raised inside a draw. Money Manager EX reported only "Unhandled unknown exception; terminating
the application" and exited 255, with no trace of what had actually happened.

A category on KTFont, in AppKit rather than CoreText because NSGraphicsContext lives here and
CoreText must not depend on AppKit. It does what -[NSFont setInContext:] does: sets the font, the
size and the text matrix on the graphics port.

MEASURED: mmex goes from a black screen to a drawn window, title bar and logo and buttons, with zero
unrecognized selectors and zero unhandled exceptions, and it stays running rather than exiting.

--- a/AppKit/NSFont.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSFont.m
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #import <Foundation/NSKeyedArchiver.h>
 
 #import <Onyx2D/O2Font.h>
+#import <CoreText/KTFont.h>
 
 NSNotificationName NSFontSetChangedNotification =
         @"NSFontSetChangedNotification";
@@ -1046,6 +1047,43 @@
 }
 
 @end
+
+/*
+ * A CTFONT UNDER NSFontAttributeName IS LEGITIMATE, and this is where that stops being a crash.
+ *
+ * kCTFontAttributeName and NSFontAttributeName are the SAME KEY, both CFSTR("NSFont"). An attributed
+ * string built through CoreText, which is what CTLineCreateWithAttributedString takes, therefore
+ * carries a CTFontRef where an AppKit one would carry an NSFont. On a Mac those are interchangeable;
+ * here KTFont and NSFont are unrelated classes.
+ *
+ * NSLayoutManager sends -setInContext: to whatever it finds under that key. A KTFont did not answer,
+ * which raised inside a draw, and Money Manager EX reported it as "Unhandled unknown exception;
+ * terminating the application" with no other trace of what had happened. Task #212.
+ *
+ * This lives in AppKit rather than CoreText because NSGraphicsContext does, and CoreText must not
+ * depend on AppKit.
+ */
+@implementation KTFont (CiderGraphicsContext)
+
+- (void) setInContext: (NSGraphicsContext *) context {
+    CGContextRef cgContext = [context graphicsPort];
+
+    if (cgContext == NULL)
+        return;
+
+    CGContextSetFont(cgContext, [self graphicsFont]);
+    CGContextSetFontSize(cgContext, [self pointSize]);
+    CGContextSetTextMatrix(cgContext,
+                           [[NSGraphicsContext currentContext] isFlipped]
+                                   ? (CGAffineTransform){1, 0, 0, -1, 0, 0}
+                                   : CGAffineTransformIdentity);
+}
+
+- (void) set {
+    [self setInContext: [NSGraphicsContext currentContext]];
+}
+
+@end
 
 #ifndef DARLING
 @implementation NSFont (PortatibilityAdditions)
